  abstract = {Deploying diverse recycling technologies is essential for advancing the circular economy in the plastic sector. This study uses a qualitative approach, combining document analysis and 25 expert interviews, to explore recycling technology deployment in the European automotive plastic sector. Five key parameters -- feedstock quality, input quantity, carbon footprint, economic performance, and output quality -- are identified as critical for technology deployment, highlighting the trade-offs between different recycling methods. Linking these trade-offs to specific automotive value chain steps -- manufacturing, collection and dismantling, shredding and post-shredding -- shows the importance of considering value chain specificities. For example, improvements in dismantling automotive plastic components could create new opportunities for mechanical and solvent-based recycling as well as depolymerization but still yield insufficient volumes for pyrolysis and gasification. A deep understanding of value chain configurations and developments is necessary to optimize technology deployment and leverage their complementarities in response to societal and environmental needs.},
