  abstract = {Futures literacy can serve not only as a pedagogical tool, but also as a collective process through which communities work with uncertainty and make shared priorities, roles, and follow-up dynamics more visible. This article examines the case of the Mar\'e favela complex in Rio de Janeiro, developed in partnership with Redes da Mar\'e, and asks how a Futures Literacy Laboratory can help convert liminal uncertainty into collective, performative agency in a vulnerable urban setting. Using participatory action research, we co-led a UNESCO-type Futures Literacy process in Mar\'e between November 2023 and July 2024, with a later follow-up phase in 2025. The empirical material includes workshop artefacts, field notes, participant reflections, and field reports from the 2024 Mar\'e Futures Week and the 2025 Futures Pollinators workshop. The results show how the process moved from the surfacing of assumptions to the reformulation of themes, the selection of priorities, and the emergence of early forms of follow-up. The article's main contribution is the Mar\'e Framework, which names the sequence that became visible through the case: Uncertainty {$\rightarrow$} Displacement {$\rightarrow$} Cognitive Openness {$\rightarrow$} Futures Exploration {$\rightarrow$} Social Recommitment. The framework is proposed as a grounded way of linking lived uncertainty, structured futures work, and the collective orientations that may remain after the workshop.},
