  abstract = {This study develops a valid technology transfer challenges in circular supply chain (CSC) for the electronic manufacturing industry in considering the absorptive capacity-dynamic capability involvement. Studies have predominantly concentrated on challenges in CSC that are largely attributed to knowledge deficiencies; yet substantial challenges relevant to strategic reconfiguration of resources and operations necessary for effective deployment of this knowledge and technology, as well as adaptation to evolving circular economy demands remain lacking. This study integrates an expanded absorptive capacity theory-dynamic capability view, emphasizing the nature of technology transfer challenges that incorporate knowledge based capacity and resource reconfiguration capability challenges needed to be addressed for effective realization of technology transfer in CSC. This study employes a hybrid method approach to extract the information from databases and use hybrid method to deal with the validity and interrelationship among the attributes. The findings emphasize the need to address circular technology knowledge acquisition challenges from potential absorptive capacity perspective and circular technology opportunity and threat sensing challenges from dynamic reconfiguration perspective to achieve realized and technology transfer. In practice, organizational and institutional inertia against circular change, management capability-circular technology gaps for operations restructuring, circular technology knowledge deficit, internal circular technology competency gaps, and poor ability to track and interpret circular regulations are the key challenges required to be addressed.},
