  urldate = {2025-11-19},
  abstract = {The accelerating demand for sustainability, coupled with rapid digitalization, has led to the emergence of Industry 5.0, which emphasizes human-centric, resilient, and sustainable industrial systems. Despite the growing recognition of digital twins (DT) as transformative enablers for supply chain innovation, limited research has integrated DT adoption with Industry 5.0 principles and circular economy objectives. Addressing this gap, this study systematically identifies and prioritizes the readiness factors required for successful DT implementation in sustainable supply chains. Using the fuzzy Best--Worst Method (fuzzy BWM), validated by expert assessments, eighteen readiness factors were evaluated, spanning technological enablers (e.g. advanced analytics, blockchain, cloud computing, cybersecurity) and organizational dimensions (e.g., leadership commitment, cultural readiness, and financial investment). The findings reveal that preparedness for DT modelling and simulation, leadership dedication, and an organizational digital mindset are the most critical enablers. The study advances theory by offering the first integrated framework that aligns Industry 5.0, DT, and circular supply chains, while contributing practical insights for managers to enhance resource efficiency, minimize waste, and strengthen resilience. By providing a prioritized roadmap of readiness factors, this research supports organizations and policymakers in accelerating the transition toward circular and sustainable supply chains in the Industry 5.0 era.},
