  abstract = {Since the late 1970's, China's transformation from a planned economy to one that is market-based and open to foreign trade and investment has fuelled economic development. Rapid economic growth has helped China to become one of the most important world economic powers, increasing the wealth of the population and providing employment and business opportunities. The rapid economic growth, however has engendered serious natural resource depletion and environmental pollution, and the continuing increase of population has exacerbated this situation. As the results of resource depletion and environmental negligence have become visible, Chinese society and decision makers have realized the seriousness of the situation. The concept of a circular economy (CE) was first proposed by scholars in China in 1998 and formally accepted in 2002 by the central government as a new development strategy that aims to alleviate the contradiction between rapid economic growth and the shortage of raw materials and energy.},
